    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Neurexins (Nrxns) are a family of three presynaptic transmembrane cell-adhesion proteins that are known to be involved in central nervous system synapse development. In recent years, mutations in NRXN genes have been linked to neuropsychiatric disorders such as schizophrenia and autism spectrum disorders, the pathogenesis of which appears to partly involve abnormalities in synapse development. However, because the fundamental role of Nrxns in the brain is poorly understood, it remains unclear why they are linked with these disorders. As synapse development in the mouse cerebral cortex occurs over the first several postnatal weeks, studying Nrxns during this time period may prove especially informative. The present study investigated the role of Nrxns in synapse development by creating a temporal expression map of the three NRXN genes in the mouse cerebral cortex by measuring mRNA levels. In addition, because NRXN2 is the least studied of the three NRXN genes, the present study focused on the synaptic role of NRXN2 by characterizing excitatory and inhibitory synapse development in a novel NRXN2 conditional knockout mouse model. Here, we show that NRXN2 exhibits a distinct expression pattern relative to NRXN1 and NRXN3, as measured by mRNA levels. Furthermore, blind analysis of puncta density, size, and intensity confocal microscopy images of cultured mouse cortical neurons was carried out, and revealed that knocking out NRXN2 expression in vitro results in an approximately 25% reduction in both an excitatory synapse marker and an inhibitory synapse marker, suggesting that NRXN2 is involved in regulating excitatory and inhibitory synapse density. The results from this study represent a step forward in understanding the synaptic role of NRXN2, which may eventually prove to be a useful foundation in the development of future therapies for neuropsychiatric disorders that NRXN mutations have been linked to.",
